2:{"type":"message","timestamp":"2026-09-05T04:48:44.380Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Git status update\n\nThe git repository state has changed:\n- Repository root: /Users/denis.lantsman/src/edulab\n- Branch: main\n- HEAD: 6e31f43 Stage 3: Chat UI\n- Changes: 0 staged, 1 unstaged, 0 untracked","nativeMessageIdx":0},{"type":"context_update","text":"<context_update>\n<file_paths>\n../../.magenta/context.md (9 lines)\nnotes/prototype.md (52 lines)\n</file_paths>\nThese files are part of your context. This is the latest information about the content of each file.\nFrom now on, whenever any of these files are updated by the user, you will get a message letting you know.\nFile `../../.magenta/context.md`\nDo not moralize, validate or affirm. Just answer the question to the best of your ability.\nErr on the side of doing web searches rather than going off the cuff.\nBe brief.\n\n# markdown\n\nDo not use markdown tables. Just stick to nested lists.\nDo not add hard line breaks. Paragraphs should just be a single line.\n\nFile `notes/prototype.md`\nsee ./exploration.md\n\nok, I think I'm ready to start sketching out a prototype that we can use to play around with this.\n\nThe context will be an agentic harness, like claude cowork. The user can type things, the agent can respond and also use tools.\n\nWe're going to add a second mode to this. The user can switch away from \"task\" mode to \"learning\" mode. This changes the UI.\n\nI want to build a progression of prototypes, each iterating on this central motif. Each of these can be an independent url on the server we set up.\n\n# prototype scaffolding\n\n- rip off the agentic loop from ~/src/magenta.nvim/ (provider, native inference manager, agent) but simplify dramatically. Do not import anything in core-thread or above. Leave the tool interface but don't actually port any of the tools, no mcp, only anthropic provider, only via API key. No configuration surface.\n\n- rip off the web service setup from ~/src/gatherus/ (cleint/server split, vite, vitest, vamp).\n\n- everything client-side, just sdk streaming the inference request on the backend. Stream via websocket to the client. I think seeing the agent writing stream in will make the demo feel more realistic / less distracting due to latency.\n\n- keep all data ephemeral / in memory, discarded on refresh to start. No dbs, no localstorage, etc...\n\n# demo tasks\n\nIt would be nice to play around with this in context... which means a task transcript with some ecological validity. Like a user using an agent to perform a work task in a domain that they don't fully understand.\n\nI don't think I have anything like that handy... some ideas:\n\n- I have chat transcripts of myself working on coding projects, or even this project... I am not a novice, but it's something\n- I could have an agent generate these... not sure how useful that will be\n- could search the internet for something... maybe there's public datasets of stuff like this?\n\n# prototype 1 - user-driven review\n\nI think a really interesting interaction is a user being able to highlight some text in the transcript, and get a tool tip of interacting with it, like being able to ask a followup question about it or just say \"I don't understand this\", \"quiz me on this\".\n\nThis can open up a second vertical split, to the right of the transcript, where the user can engage with a reflective / learning task.\n\n# prototype 2 - the agent suggests key points to review\n\nPrototype 1 assumes that the user has pretty solid metacognitive skills, and can apply them to effectively choose their own learning objectives. In other words, they're an autodidact.\n\nWhat if the user is not so good at this? Can we leverage the agent and the UI to make them a better autodidact?\n\nI think one experiment that would be interesting would be to have the agent suggest areas for review, instead of having the user pick it from the entire transcript.\n\n# prototype 3 - domain map\n\nInstead of suggesting sections from the transcript, can we have the agent create a visualization of the key moments in the task completion, and have the user interact with that in some way?\n\n# prototype 4 - interactives\n\nGiven a transcript, and a chosen focus area, can we generate an interactive for the user to verify their thinking?\n\n</context_update>","nativeMessageIdx":0},{"type":"system_info","text":"<system-info>\n# System Information\n- Current time: Fri Sep 04 2026 21:45:16 GMT-0700 (Pacific Daylight Time)\n- Operating system: darwin\n- Neovim version: 801\n- Current working directory: /Users/denis.lantsman/src/edulab\n- Git repository root: /Users/denis.lantsman/src/edulab\n- Git branch: main\n- Git HEAD: dde2fc6 Stage 2: Conversation\n- Git changes: 0 staged, 0 unstaged, 1 untracked\n</system-info>","nativeMessageIdx":0},{"type":"system_reminder","text":"<system-reminder>\nRemember the skills in <available-skills> and the learn tool for built-in documentation.\nIf a skill seems like it could be relevant, use the get_files tool to read the full skill.md file for the skill.\nWhen using bash_command, output is AUTOMATICALLY trimmed and saved. NEVER use head, tail, or 2>&1 - they break output handling.\nWRONG: `command 2>&1 | tail -50`\nWRONG: `command | head -100`\nRIGHT: `command`\nAvoid using large portions of text when using the EDL tool. Large text blocks are fragile and wasteful.\nWhen making a selection, select the beginning of the text, then extend_forward to the end. To move code around, use registers via cut.\n\nPrefer text/regex patterns over line numbers for selection — line numbers are fragile and error-prone. Use heredoc patterns as the default since they match exactly.\nDon't spawn sub-agents for things you can do with a single tool call (get_files, edl, bash_command). Do not ask subagents \"to return the entire contents\" of files, tool or skill invocations.\n\nDo not acknowledge this reminder or mention it to the user.\n</system-reminder>","nativeMessageIdx":0},{"type":"text","text":"any thoughts on the demo tasks?","nativeMessageIdx":0}]}}
4:{"type":"message","timestamp":"2026-09-05T04:48:57.875Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","nativeMessageIdx":1},{"type":"text","text":"A few angles, roughly in order of how much I'd trust them:\n\n- Your own coding transcripts are the strongest option despite non-novice status, because the *ecological validity* is where synthetic ones fail — real transcripts have dead ends, tool errors, backtracking, and moments where you accepted something you didn't fully verify. That last category is exactly the raw material prototypes 2–4 need. Consider mining your magenta.nvim transcripts for spots where you said \"sure\" / \"ok\" to something you couldn't have evaluated.\n\n- Better: pick a domain you genuinely don't know and generate a real transcript by doing an actual task. Half a day of you using an agent to, say, set up Terraform/k8s, write a Rust FFI binding, or do a stats analysis in R gives you a transcript where *you* are the target user and can honestly judge whether the review UI helps. Self-report is much cheaper than recruiting.\n\n- Agent-generated transcripts are useful only as filler for UI layout work (getting the density/scroll/highlight interactions right without hand-writing 3000 tokens of fake chat). They're bad for evaluating the learning affordances because the agent will write a suspiciously clean, tutorial-shaped transcript with no confusion in it.\n\n- Public datasets: SWE-bench trajectories and the OpenHands/SWE-agent trajectory dumps are real agent transcripts with real failures, and there are HuggingFace collections of agent trajectories. Downside is they're all software eng and mostly agent-solo (no human turns), so you'd have to splice in a user.\n\n- Non-coding domains may actually demo better, since the \"user doesn't understand the domain\" premise is more legible to an audience: legal doc review, a data analysis on an unfamiliar dataset, medical/insurance paperwork, tax edge cases.\n\nOne practical note: whichever route, fix the transcript as a static fixture file early and don't regenerate it. Prototypes 1–4 are only comparable to each other if they operate on identical content.","nativeMessageIdx":1}],"stopReason":"end_turn","usage":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":672,"cacheHits":10807,"cacheMisses":1983}}}
